      goal: "In Python, read the energy log, convert air to scfm (scf an hour over 60), and fit kW against scfm with a straight line using numpy's polyfit.",
      code: `import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import pyodbc

WEEK_START, WEEK_END = "2026-10-31 06:00", "2026-11-07 06:00"
conn = pyodbc.connect(
    "DRIVER={ODBC Driver 18 for SQL Server};"
    "SERVER=plant-sql01;DATABASE=PlantDB;"
    "Trusted_Connection=yes;TrustServerCertificate=yes;"
)
log = pd.read_sql("SELECT * FROM energy_log ORDER BY hour_end", conn, parse_dates=["hour_end"])
log = log.astype({c: float for c in log.columns if c != "hour_end"})
log["scfm"] = log["air_scf"] / 60
slope, intercept = np.polyfit(log["scfm"], log["kwh"], 1)
print(f"{len(log)} hours: kW = {intercept:.2f} + {slope:.4f} x scfm")
print(f"Specific power {slope * 100:.1f} kW per 100 scfm; {intercept:.1f} kW at no flow")`,
      about: "Specific power is how much electricity the compressor needs per unit of air: 16.2 kW per 100 scfm here, about what a well-loaded rotary screw compressor does. The intercept, 21.5 kW at no flow, is what it draws just running unloaded. Both matter: the slope prices each scfm saved, and the intercept is why a compressor left running over a shutdown still costs money. A kWh in one hour is the average kW over that hour, so kWh and kW are the same number here. Meter values are illustrative; the stops and barrels are canonical.",
      terms: [
        ["np.polyfit", "np.polyfit(x, y, 1)", "Least-squares straight line: slope and intercept."],
        ["slope", "Specific power", "kW per scfm."],
        ["/ 60", "scfm", "Cubic feet an hour over 60 minutes."],
      ],
      out: `336 hours: kW = 21.48 + 0.1617 x scfm
Specific power 16.2 kW per 100 scfm; 21.5 kW at no flow`,
